8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-21T14:42:59.091Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;pIrZRHDwmPkCM_i7w3aoBWqRin-7m7YGqeAMgEgZUKMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxqhttps://theconversation.com/why-people-are-ordering-fake-food-and-fashion-deliveries-on-free-dopamine-apps-287690ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Wmd-cMF7bAdXUQ_9iMf9kQMFmdRhU7nGU6ufxp6WPWwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;PxoAzPDirsKziIDEz_DCADhh2RWz1p14a2uVuiBqAusƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;9zUR0K3IMf8kUl2ZGRWjvL4LiJaGpVbbea-U1MZKg3cƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxqhttps://theconversation.com/why-people-are-ordering-fake-food-and-fashion-deliveries-on-free-dopamine-apps-287690ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;w1CSmVOf3aOA1cUxZgf27DpZ0c6xTO5iI_PSg7icso8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky ƒ<figure><img src="https://images.theconversation.com/files/748770/original/file-20260720-57-djjo5k.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.1&amp;rect=0%2C1%2C6720%2C4480&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=1050&amp;h=700&amp;fit=crop" /><figcaption><span class="caption"></span> <span class="attribution"><span class="source">Cottonbro Studio/Pexels</span>, <a class="license" href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY</a></span></figcaption></figure><p>Itâ€™s Saturday night and youâ€™ve downloaded a new food delivery app. Youâ€™ve scanned the menus, picked some Mexican dishes, placed your order and are now tracking the delivery. But your food never comes â€“ and you never expected it to.</p> <p>It sounds ridiculous if youâ€™ve never tried it, but this viral trend is more popular than you might think. Just one app alone, FoodNeverComes, claims nearly 1 million â€œ<a href="https://foodnevercomes.com/">cravings satisfied</a>â€ to date.</p> <p>Why would people bother spending time on apps or websites that are built to never deliver? Ultimately, itâ€™s all about getting a <a href="https://www.health.harvard.edu/mind-and-mood/dopamine-the-pathway-to-pleasure">dopamine</a> hit.</p> <p>It sounds like harmless fun, letting users â€œbuyâ€ food, clothes or other products with no real-world cost, since most of these apps are free to use. </p> <p>But even when an app or website costs you nothing, there are downsides to watch out for â€“ including how your choices can help advertisers better target you in real life.</p> <p></p> <h2>From South Korea to the world</h2> <p><a href="https://foodnevercomes.com/">FoodNeverComes</a> is one example of a free, ad-supported app and website that mimics an online food delivery service. But there are other types too. </p> <p>For instance, the free, ad-supported <a href="https://dopamine-shop.com/?p=115">Dopamine Shop</a> is a fake online shopping site. There, customers view items â€“ everything from a 99 cent pet rock to a $99 million moon â€“ then fill their carts. But no payment details are requested and <a href="https://dopamine-shop.com/about/">no transaction ever occurs</a>. Nothing is ever shipped.</p> <p>There are even fake, free â€œsmokingâ€ apps such as No Smoke Zone, also known as Virtual Smoke, which describes itself like this:</p> <blockquote> <p>Virtual Smoke is a realistic cigarette simulation app that lets you enjoy the look, feel, and relaxation of smoking â€“ without any nicotine or harmful effects. Perfect for those trying to quit, reduce stress, or just have fun with a virtual cigarette.</p> </blockquote> <p>These new platforms are whatâ€™s known as â€œ<a href="https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/lifestyle/trends/20260527/gen-z-turn-to-dopamine-sites-for-quick-comfort">dopamine apps</a>â€ or â€œdopamine sitesâ€.</p> <p></p> <p>While they began <a href="https://edition.cnn.com/2026/06/24/world/video/dopamine-sites-in-south-korea-feature-fake-cigarettes-and-food-deliveries-digvid-hnk">in South Korea</a>, interestingly, Dopamine Shop is owned and developed by <a href="https://dopamine-shop.com/about/">Fenwick Holdings LLC</a>, a small independent studio in the United States. This suggests this new genre of apps and websites may be spreading globally.</p> <h2>Why does it feel so good?</h2> <p><a href="https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/22581-dopamine">Dopamine</a> is both a powerful <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539894/">neurotransmitter</a> and <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/cells10040735">neuromodulator</a> â€“ delivering chemical messages and sparking changes in your brain.</p> <p>Dopamine plays <a href="https://www.health.harvard.edu/mind-and-mood/dopamine-the-pathway-to-pleasure">several important roles</a>, including motivating us toward goals and helping your brain learn which actions lead to beneficial outcomes. </p> <p>With these new dopamine platforms, users experience:</p> <ul> <li>anticipation (â€œI might find something goodâ€)</li> <li>selection (â€œI control my choicesâ€)</li> <li>and a feeling of confirmation (â€œMy order is placed!â€).</li> </ul> <p>The only element of the process missing, compared to a real app or website, is consumption (â€œI received itâ€).</p> <p>Even before these latest apps, some people had already realised they can get a similar retail therapy rush by <a href="https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/retail/amazon-wish-list">creating wish lists</a> or filling their cart, then deleting it, <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/nobuy/comments/1ikee7l/does_anyone_else_fill_an_online_shopping_cart_and/?solution=3905623f40e038483905623f40e03848&amp;js_challenge=1&amp;token=7afd7253fec22262ff1c52b1703fe9ecadff218b421453b55f70f9441da279bc&amp;jsc_orig_r=">without making a purchase</a>.</p> <p>You can simulate the same feeling by creating <a href="https://sproutsocial.com/glossary/pinterest-board/">Pinterest boards</a> of your dream home, without building a thing. Or you can imagine your dream holiday, using various free or paid travel planning apps and websites.</p> <h2>Potential as a â€˜gateway behaviourâ€™</h2> <p>Could pretending to order food, shopping or having â€œfun with a virtual cigaretteâ€ have any downsides?</p> <p>These dopamine apps and websites are relatively new, so we donâ€™t yet have specific research on how much, if at all, they might serve as â€œgateway behavioursâ€. A <a href="https://www.bethechangeconsulting.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/BTC4b_interrupt-gateway-behaviors_color.pdf">gateway behaviour</a> is an initial behaviour that opens a pathway toward later behaviours becoming more likely.</p> <p>However, this is an area thatâ€™s been well studied before in other areas, such as gambling and gaming. And that past research raises potential concerns â€“ particularly since these dopamine apps involve some level of gamification. </p> <p>For example, a notable <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-014-9511-0">2014 study</a> followed 409 people who played <a href="https://www.abc.net.au/news/2022-03-01/social-casino-games-and-why-people-become-addicted/100861446">social casino games</a>, but who had never gambled online with real money. Six months later, about 26% had migrated to real online gambling.</p> <p>A <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2016.05.021">2016 study</a> of 521 social casino users found 19% reported gambling for real money as a direct result of social casino gaming.</p> <p>And a 2018 <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/S10899-018-9755-1">study of 1,178 German teens</a>, aged 11 to 19, found that engagement with simulated gambling predicted subsequent real money gambling one year later.</p> <h2>How your â€˜cookiesâ€™ feed targeted ads</h2> <p>Remember the old saying, â€œthereâ€™s no such thing as a free lunchâ€?</p> <p>While these apps and websites might be fun and free, your fake choices can still be incredibly valuable for marketers.</p> <p>Free apps often rely on advertising to pay for making their content free. Apps or websites that use <a href="https://adsense.google.com/start/">Google AdSense</a> to display their ads use <a href="https://support.google.com/adsense/answer/7549925?hl=en">cookies</a>: tiny text files <a href="https://foodnevercomes.com/privacy.html">stored on your browser</a> to track your online browsing habits. </p> <p>Third-party advertisers, including Google, can then use your history to serve you more targeted ads. (If you read the fine print, <a href="https://dopamine-shop.com/privacy/">sites note</a> how to <a href="https://foodnevercomes.com/privacy.html">opt out of personalised advertising</a>.)</p> <p>If you do purchase that pretend jacket, or order that delivery of fake tacos, donâ€™t be surprised if you start seeing more ads for the real thing.</p><img src="https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287690/count.gif" alt="The Conversation" width="1" height="1" /> <p class="fine-print"><em><span>Gary Mortimer has received past funding from the Building Employer Confidence and Inclusion in Disability Grant, the AusIndustry Entrepreneurs&#39; Program, the National Clothing Textiles Stewardship Scheme, the National Retail Association and the Australian Retailers Association. He is an independent director and board chair of Services and Creative Skills Australia, a federally-funded jobs and skills council.</span></em></p>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;SycVXA3LWXm7SAYsaXyzP-Eq2ByAr3VLnHX1DrsQsTUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;FkcPROl2Ze5bd4R2DmhV5g07Vsg57zuV6b379pX1Ikcƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;7EA3HPj2JRTUT0SqajpE3cAxPtv0h95LwjcXcNGgrEwƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;670X9l_kHYqk2HaYIQbUprKrv3UL3C5Z1ZXZly_Z2ngƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;14Rxhy6fO6T6I6XHxPXgvDXHH4irjbUO5uqNRGeaaqUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;iAOMqpl2yPgLp0mswn6veBaVdhLmIwxzDVSLttkABA8ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;EnZSLLUP3TNh8LaxbcQ3BeqxgAyaEiqO-njaApSMQHAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkdTextƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;WByjmvnFGBq3r9ayHMnx5WsDsy7AlgndlqmMWdgi6lEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqSyndicationSourceƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;YWPgdtqe2ZC8b7ydkIsyZ1epSUGhn8ixcNZ4maiBSO8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx(urn:rmn:predicate:source-reports-summaryƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;L-ptH-7vcz-hlRk6UH50rzIFLt-1saAk0UrWCTpb80Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarktUnsaturatedPredicateƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CafrZYajU3VwL_wIEMT3hjYGas2RWohQkDmo_p8AH8oƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarksGroundedPredicationƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;9pdm3Tbpo2sI4HpWwry3JrW206QMhYr4pEqgsE9l0goƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚